---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { languagePaths, type LanguageProps } from "../../../i18n/routes";

export const getStaticPaths = languagePaths;
const { lang } = Astro.props as LanguageProps;
---
<Base
  title="ComfyUI for local AI — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="LightPhotos now has generative fill: paint over something and a ComfyUI server on your own computer fills it in. No cloud, no account, and no model inside the app."
  active="blogs"
  lang={lang}
  path="/blogs/comfyui-for-local-ai.html"
  translated={false}
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-11-11">November 11, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>ComfyUI for local AI</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          I promised never to send your photos to a cloud AI. Generative fill
          still sounded useful. ComfyUI lets me have both: the model runs on
          your own computer, next to your photos.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 4 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          Paint over a power line, a stranger or a sign, and LightPhotos
          asks ComfyUI on your computer for three ways to fill it. The model
          is a 6.9 GB download. Nothing leaves your computer, and the fill is
          an ordinary edit that you can undo, grade and export.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>Why ComfyUI</h2>
      <p>
        In <a href="/blogs/my-plan.html">my plan</a>, I said no to cloud AI
        and maybe to ComfyUI. Cloud AI sends your photo to servers that
        another company controls. I won&rsquo;t do that.
      </p>
      <p>
        Building image generation into LightPhotos would be worse in a
        different way. The app would grow by gigabytes, I would have to keep
        GPU code working on four platforms, and you would be stuck with my
        choice of model.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="https://www.comfy.org">ComfyUI</a> already does the hard
        part. It runs image models on Mac, Windows and Linux, on whatever
        GPU you have, and it is free and open source. It has an HTTP API on your own computer. So
        LightPhotos generates nothing itself. It sends the area you painted
        to ComfyUI and keeps what comes back.
      </p>

      <h2>Setting it up</h2>
      <ol class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          Install <a href="https://www.comfy.org/download">ComfyUI
          Desktop</a> and start it. A ComfyUI that you run from source works
          too.
        </li>
        <li>
          In LightPhotos, open Settings and turn on <strong>Enable
          ComfyUI</strong>. It is off by default. While it is off,
          LightPhotos never contacts a server.
        </li>
        <li>
          Leave <strong>Server</strong> empty. LightPhotos tries ComfyUI
          Desktop&rsquo;s port, 8000, and then 8188, on
          <code>127.0.0.1</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Check <strong>Models folder</strong>. LightPhotos reads the folder
          that ComfyUI Desktop is set to, so it is usually filled in already.
        </li>
        <li>
          Under <strong>Generative Fill</strong>, click
          <strong>Download</strong> for each model file.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        LightPhotos ships no model weights. Generative Fill uses SDXL
        Inpainting 0.1, as four files from Hugging Face: the UNet, two text
        encoders and the VAE, 6.9 GB together. Each one downloads to a
        <code>.part</code> file and is renamed into place only when its size
        and SHA-256 match, so ComfyUI never sees half a model. If the
        download stops, it continues where it stopped.
      </p>

      <h2>Using it</h2>
      <p>
        Open a photo, go to Touch Up and click <strong>+ Brush</strong>.
        Paint over what you want gone and let go. That&rsquo;s it.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Prompt</strong> is optional. Leave it empty and the fill
          continues the area around it. Type &ldquo;grass&rdquo; or
          &ldquo;blue sky&rdquo; to steer it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Each generation makes <strong>three variations</strong>, from three
          seeds. Step through them with <code>&lsaquo; 1/3 &rsaquo;</code>,
          or click <strong>Generate More</strong> for three new ones.
        </li>
        <li>
          A pin marks each fill on the photo. Click it to pick it again
          later, or to delete it.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        When something is missing, the button is greyed out, not hidden,
        and its hover text names the first thing to fix: turn it on in
        Settings, choose the models folder, download a file, or start
        ComfyUI. When ComfyUI starts, the button turns on by itself within
        five seconds.
      </p>

      <h2>A fill is just another edit</h2>
      <p>
        This is the part that took the most work, and the part I care about
        most. A generated patch isn&rsquo;t pasted into your photo:
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Your photo file is never changed.</strong> Each variation
          is saved as a small image file beside the photo. The sidecar keeps
          about 300 bytes saying where the fill goes and which variation you
          picked.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The sliders still work.</strong> The fill is blended in
          early, before white balance, exposure and local edits. If you
          change the exposure afterwards, the fill changes with the rest of
          the photo.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It&rsquo;s everywhere the photo is.</strong> Thumbnails,
          the Loupe and every export show the fill, and undo removes it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It doesn&rsquo;t need ComfyUI anymore.</strong> Once a fill
          is made, it draws and exports with ComfyUI closed, or on a computer
          that never had it. The browser version of LightPhotos can&rsquo;t
          make fills, but it shows them.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What LightPhotos sends</h2>
      <p>
        Only a crop. LightPhotos cuts out the painted area with some space
        around it, scales it to 1024 pixels or less, and sends that, with
        the painted part made transparent. ComfyUI never gets the whole
        photo, the file name or the metadata. ComfyUI keeps the crops it
        receives in its own <code>input</code> folder, so empty that folder
        if you don&rsquo;t want them kept.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightPhotos asks the server only while Touch Up is open, every five
        seconds while it is down and every 30 seconds while it is up. If
        ComfyUI stops halfway, the error says what to do: &ldquo;ComfyUI
        stopped responding at 127.0.0.1:8000. Start it again, then press
        Generate.&rdquo; A generation that gets one or two variations keeps
        them.
      </p>

      <h2>The limits</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Desktop only.</strong> Generating a fill needs the desktop
          app, in v0.1.40.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>RAW highlights.</strong> A model works on ordinary display
          images. In a RAW file, very bright highlights inside a filled area
          clip to white.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One model, for now.</strong> The workflow is built in. It
          is plain ComfyUI workflow JSON, and LightPhotos only needs three
          things from it: one image input, one image output, and a text node
          titled <code>LightPhotos Prompt</code>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What&rsquo;s next</h2>
      <p>
        Generative Fill is the first row in a table of AI features. Each row
        is a workflow and the model files it needs. Settings, the downloads
        and the server check all read the table, so the next feature is
        mostly a new workflow, not new plumbing.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rule stays the same: AI in LightPhotos runs on your computer,
        or not at all.
      </p>

      <nav class="lp-article-nav" aria-label="More posts">
        <a class="lp-article-nav-prev" href="/blogs/adding-gps-to-my-full-frame-camera.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Back</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Adding GPS to my full-frame camera</span>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
